      "id": "lem-arith-connected-smooth-quasiprojective-model-proper-special-fibre",
      "concern": "Statement: 'If the special fibre G_k is proper and geometrically connected, then G is proper over R.' Nonemptiness is absent, although Proof 3.1 explicitly uses 'nonempty'. The opened supplier def-geometrically-reduced-integral-connected-fibre declares the empty geometric fibre connected. Take R=C[[t]], an elliptic curve E/C, and G=E×_C Spec C((t)), viewed over R. This is smooth, separated, finite type, quasi-projective and connected, with abelian generic fibre and empty proper geometrically connected special fibre, but is not proper over R. The Néron–Ogg–Shafarevich consumer uses the identity subgroup, whose unit guarantees nonemptiness; that use does not supply the missing hypothesis in this lemma.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "lem-arith-hilbert-divisor-charts-and-picard-diagonal",
      "concern": "Statement (b) asserts a 'smooth projective-space bundle ... on arbitrary tests' for general projective geometrically integral A, without assuming a rational point. Proof 2.1 replaces an arbitrary Picard-sheaf test class by an actual line bundle L and identifies its fibre with P((p_*L)∨); a sheaf class need not admit such a representative on that test. For a nonsplit real conic, the geometric degree-d class for odd sufficiently large d is a real point of the fppf Picard sheaf, but its effective-divisor fibre has no real point: every real effective divisor has even degree. It therefore cannot be a projective space over R. The fibre is a Brauer–Severi form. The relevant distinction and descent argument were checked in [Liedtke, Theorem 3.4 and Remark 3.5](https://math.nyu.edu/~tschinke/books/simons15/submitted/brauerseveri5.pdf), PDF pp. 13–15. Adjudication should determine whether 'projective-space bundle' was intended only fppf-locally; the stated all-test formula requires a qualification. The owned Picard-representation consumer specializes to abelian varieties with an identity point, where this obstruction vanishes.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "deformation-theory-of-schemes-and-obstruction-spaces-examples",
      "concern": "Page line 18 identifies the conic Hilbert component as P(S_2). The opened projective-bundle definition uses the quotient convention, and the assigned example correctly identifies the coefficient-line parameter scheme as P(S_2∨). The page should preserve that dual convention; the dimension P^5 is unchanged.",
      "severity": "presentation"
    }
